<%--
Test_Modal_Form

The modal-form worked example: a component whose template is an <Rsx_Form>, and
nothing else. It has NO JavaScript class, which is the standard case - the modal is
chrome, it finds this form and drives its submit(), and the endpoint is named exactly
once, on the form tag.

$data (optional) seed values, for the pre-filled (edit) variant of the demo. A create
form simply omits it and starts pristine.

Note what is NOT here: no vals() delegate, no render_error() delegate, no controller
call, and no client-side checking of name/email/role. Every one of those rules is the
SERVER's, and the server's error comes back and renders in <Form_Errors /> and under
the fields it names.
--%>
<Define:Test_Modal_Form>

    <Rsx_Form $controller="Dev_Modals_Controller" $method="register_user" $data=this.args.data>

        <div class="mb-4">
            <h5 class="text-muted mb-3">User Information</h5>

            <%-- Failure feedback renders HERE, because the author placed it here. --%>
            <Form_Errors />

            <Form_Field $label="Full Name" $required=true>
                <Text_Input $name="name" $max_length=255 $placeholder="Enter your full name" $autofocus=true />
            </Form_Field>

            <Form_Field $label="Email Address" $required=true>
                <Text_Input $name="email" $type="email" $max_length=255 $placeholder="user@example.com" />
            </Form_Field>

            <% const _role_options = [
                {value: 'admin', label: 'Administrator'},
                {value: 'manager', label: 'Manager'},
                {value: 'user', label: 'User'},
                {value: 'guest', label: 'Guest'},
            ]; %>
            <Form_Field $label="Role" $required=true>
                <Select_Input $name="role" $options=_role_options $placeholder="-- Select a role --" />
            </Form_Field>
        </div>

    </Rsx_Form>

</Define:Test_Modal_Form>
